00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.210
PHILIP MAIER: Hi, Philip here.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.249
I'm a technical curriculum
developer here at Google.

00:00:13.249 --> 00:00:15.040
HARISH BRAHMSANDRA:
And hi from me, Harish.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.930
I'm a lab architect at Google.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.030
On this episode, we'll be
looking at Google Cloud

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:23.020
SDK, a set of tools including
command line tools like GCloud,

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.990
bq, and GS [INAUDIBLE] to
help manage your resources

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.345
and applications on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:28.345 --> 00:00:29.720
PHILIP MAIER:
We'll also take you

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.060
through a quick demo
of a self-paced lab

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.270
where we'll install and
initialize Cloud SDK

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.910
and run commands using
GCloud, one of many tools

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.130
that come packaged
with Cloud SDK,

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.434
and the primary
command line interface

00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:43.475
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:48.659 --> 00:00:51.200
HARISH BRAHMSANDRA: Google Cloud
Console already provides you

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.770
with a powerful browser-based
UI to manage the GCP product

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.080
and services that
drive your cloud apps.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.080
With the Cloud SDK, you
can manipulate and develop

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.960
against your Cloud resources
using command line.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.050
It often provides access to
more options than the console,

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.870
but it also makes it possible
for you to script your actions

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.034
as well as log and audit them.

00:01:11.034 --> 00:01:12.700
PHILIP MAIER: You can
also use Cloud SDK

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.740
to access App Engine,
Compute Engine, Cloud Storage

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.310
BigQuery, and every other GCP
product from the command line.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.392
And you have the choice to
run these tools interactively

00:01:22.392 --> 00:01:23.600
or through automated scripts.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.667
bq is another tool available
in Cloud SDK for BigQuery.

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:35.000
HARISH BRAHMSANDRA: You can
use bq you to create and get

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.840
information about resources,
list and update resources, load

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.200
and query data, use
external data sources,

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.699
export data, and use the
BigQuery data transfer service.

00:01:45.699 --> 00:01:47.240
PHILIP MAIER: So
that's a quick taste

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.820
of some of the key tools
and functionalities offered

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.380
through Cloud SDK.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.090
Looking to install
and initialize

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:56.210
Cloud SDK on the operating
system of your choice?

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.340
Click this link for info
on how to get started.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.550
In this lab, you'll learn
to install the Cloud SDK,

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.900
initialize it, and
run core GCloud

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.990
commands from the command
line on a virtual machine.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:14.540
The Cloud SDK RPM
packages are supported

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.739
for Red Hat Enterprise
Linux 7 and CentOS 7.

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:19.280
HARISH BRAHMSANDRA:
You can check out

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.240
the link to start
the quick lab here,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.550
which will take about
30 minutes to complete.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.425
So far in the lab
we have created a VM

00:02:26.425 --> 00:02:27.300
with a Red Hat image.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.260
We then SSH to the VM.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.580
We update the Cloud
SDK RPM packages.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:49.100
We initialize the SDK,
authenticating when prompted.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.180
We can now list accounts
whose credentials

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.560
are stored in the VM.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:13.590
We can list the properties in
our active SDK configuration.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.370
We can view information on
the active SDK configuration.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.310
We can view GCloud
command help, as well as

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:21.585
specific help for a command.

00:03:24.024 --> 00:03:25.690
PHILIP MAIER: So that
brings our episode

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.800
on Cloud SDK to a close.

